.TH GIT-STAGE-BATCH-REWRITE 1 "August 2026" "git-stage-batch @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
git-stage-batch-rewrite \- execute deterministic history refinements
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B git-stage-batch rewrite scan
[\fB\-\-output\fR \fIFILE\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
[\fIBOUNDARY\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite validate
[\fB\-\-workspace\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fIPLAN\fR
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite resolve
\fIPLAN\fR
\fB\-\-workspace\fR \fIDIR\fR
[\fB\-\-accept\fR]
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite apply
[\fB\-\-workspace\fR \fIDIR\fR]
[\fB\-\-allow-published-ref\fR \fIREF\fR]...
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
\fIPLAN\fR
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite status
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite continue
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite abort
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.PP
.B git-stage-batch rewrite verify
[\fB\-\-porcelain\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rewrite scan
captures an immutable, linear commit range and emits a semantic KEEP plan
template. The snapshot contains full commit, parent, and tree object IDs;
exact author, committer, message, encoding, and signature state; and stable
patch-unit identities derived from streamed zero-context diffs. It also
records compact per-unit backward-commutation evidence: original position,
earliest proven position, and the first BLOCKED or UNKNOWN barrier. Consecutive
different-path crossings are verified as one exact block replay, while
same-path units retain individual dependency evidence. Analysis resumes after
an unsupported commit boundary but retains an UNKNOWN edge across it.
.PP
Patch bytes are not copied into JSON. Each commit records its exact old and
new tree pair, from which later commands regenerate full-index binary patches
through bounded storage.
.PP
.B rewrite validate
strictly parses a reviewed scan document, independently reacquires every
immutable source fact, and accepts KEEP, REWORD, SPLIT, INTEGRATE, and
mechanically proven REORDER outputs. A cached fact is reused only after its
exact source inputs and object closure still match. Validation requires exact
source and patch-unit conservation, original target authorship,
dependency-safe ordering, complete replay, and the frozen final tree.
For a plan whose outputs all use EXACT materialization, omit
.BR \-\-workspace ;
a gratuitous workspace is rejected rather than ignored. A plan containing any
RESOLVED output requires a COMPLETE private workspace supplied with
.BR \-\-workspace .
Validation authenticates that workspace and its completion record read-only,
replays its exact and resolved outputs in a fresh object quarantine, and
rechecks the frozen final tree. No candidate objects or refs persist.
.PP
.B rewrite resolve
creates or advances a private external workspace for explicitly RESOLVED
outputs. It replays from the frozen base in a temporary object quarantine,
exports one immutable request at a time with current-parent and source
references, and accepts only unit-authorized result paths and transitions.
Each accepted output receives a digest-bound receipt. Completion requires the
entire replay to reproduce the frozen final tree and does not create commits,
refs, or persistent candidate objects.
The completed external workspace remains a reusable, read-only input to
.BR "rewrite validate" .
Supply it with
.B \-\-workspace
on the initial
.B rewrite apply
invocation to hand its authenticated results to a durable operation. Apply
does not modify or publish the external workspace itself.
.PP
.B rewrite apply
requires
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
on the initial invocation for a plan containing any RESOLVED output.
.I DIR
must be the COMPLETE private workspace produced by
.B rewrite resolve
for that exact plan. Omit the option for an all-EXACT plan; a gratuitous,
missing, incomplete, changed, or differently bound workspace is rejected.
.PP
Before activation, apply authenticates the external workspace, copies its
exact private inventory into the unpublished operation directory, confirms
that the source did not change during the copy, and independently
authenticates the copy in a separate fresh object quarantine. It then rechecks
the branch, index, and worktree mutation facts. Once activated, building,
continue, status, and verify use only the operation-owned copy. The external
workspace may then be moved or deleted without affecting continuation or
later verification; its path is not supplied again.
.PP
Apply creates a durable operation-owned recovery ref and PREPARED checkpoint,
then reconstructs exact and resolved output trees and builds deterministic
unsigned commits in a temporary object quarantine. It durably promotes the
required commit, tree, and blob closure before updating the local operation-owned
output ref. A promotion lease and exact-name ref updates keep interruption
recovery bounded to operation-owned objects and refs.
.PP
Before changing the checked-out branch, apply requires the complete output
closure to be readable from persistent object storage with lazy fetching
disabled. It independently regenerates the expected commits and trees in a
fresh quarantine, compares them with the persistent objects, and writes a
digest-bound verification record. For a resolved plan, that record also binds
the raw-plan and completion digests and the resolved-output count. Apply then
updates the branch once with compare-and-swap. Commit objects preserve the
planned author, target source committer, declared encoding, and exact message
bytes. Source signature headers are omitted and recorded by digest.
.PP
Apply does not contact a remote and never performs or authorizes a push or
force-push.
.PP
.B rewrite status
reports the closed phase, exact next action, output progress, recovery ref,
and independently rechecked resume preconditions for a durable operation.
Before the apply executor creates an operation, status reports that no
operation is active. After completion or abort, it reports the latest terminal
operation while leaving the active slot available for another plan.
Porcelain status includes counts by persisted plan operation, allowing a
specialized workflow to verify the active plan kind without reading private
checkpoint files.
.PP
The porcelain
.B inspection.resolution_matches
member is null only for a captured, digest-bound, structurally valid all-EXACT
output plan with no resolution provenance. It is true only when a plan with
RESOLVED outputs has matching provenance and its operation-owned workspace
authenticates. An invalid output plan, missing or gratuitous provenance, a
provenance mismatch, or a changed workspace reports false, adds the
resolution-bundle-changed blocker, and prevents continuation.
.PP
.B rewrite continue
revalidates an active checkpoint and executes only its exact next action.
Pending output publication and final branch publication are reconciled
idempotently after interruption.
.PP
.B rewrite abort
records restore intent, retracts a pending output ref, and restores the
original branch only when the live tip is an exact value owned by the
operation. Foreign movement is never overwritten.
.PP
.B rewrite verify
independently reconstructs the active or latest complete output, rechecks all
commit metadata and topology, and compares its deterministic audit record to
the checkpoint digest. It may run while another worktree owns a staging
session, but takes the repository session lock so its related reads cannot
interleave with rewrite apply, continue, or abort mutations.
.SH SCAN OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-output " FILE"
Atomically write the reusable JSON document to
.IR FILE .
Existing regular-file permissions are preserved; symbolic-link destinations
are refused.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Write the complete reusable JSON document to standard output. This can be
combined with
.B \-\-output
when both a file and standard output are useful.
.TP
.I BOUNDARY
Commit excluded from the source range. By default, use the fork point or
merge base between HEAD and its configured upstream.
.SH VALIDATE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
Authenticate the COMPLETE private rewrite-resolution workspace at
.I DIR
for a plan containing RESOLVED outputs. The workspace must be bound to the
exact plan and is inspected without repair or advancement. Supplying this
option for an all-EXACT plan is rejected.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON validation report with commit/unit counts, final tree,
signature count, freshly collected mutation preconditions, and
.B summary.resolved_outputs
count. The top-level
.B resolution
member is null for an all-EXACT plan; completed-workspace validation reports
its canonical path, authenticated
.B complete_sha256
digest, and resolved-output count.
.SH RESOLVE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
Create a new private resolution workspace at
.IR DIR .
The first invocation requires a plan containing at least one RESOLVED output
and requires that
.I DIR
does not exist. Subsequent invocations reopen only the workspace bound to the
same plan.
.TP
.B \-\-accept
Validate and accept the current editable result, then stop at the next
RESOLVED output or publish the completed workspace marker. Without this
option, a seeded or edited result is never accepted.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON checkpoint containing the canonical plan and workspace
paths, current zero-based output index, opaque output key, authorized Git
paths, and exact request/result locations.
.PP
Edit only the named
.B result.json
and opaque result artifacts. JSON object-member order is insignificant. A
present path requires its opaque artifact and an authorized mode of 100644 or
100755. An authorized deletion uses ABSENT with a null mode and removes its
result artifact; an authorized addition becomes PRESENT after its artifact is
created. Every listed path must change, and selected source units must
authorize each content, lifecycle, or mode transition. Workspace directories
must remain mode 0700, and every metadata or artifact file must remain mode
0600.
.SH STATUS OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print versioned JSON containing the complete durable checkpoint inspection.
.SH APPLY OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-workspace " DIR"
Authenticate and take an operation-owned copy of the COMPLETE private
rewrite-resolution workspace at
.I DIR
when starting a plan containing RESOLVED outputs. The workspace must be bound
to the exact plan. Supplying this option for an all-EXACT plan is rejected.
After activation, continue, status, and verify use the owned copy and no
longer depend on
.IR DIR .
.TP
.BI \-\-allow-published-ref " REF"
Permit local rewriting when a source commit is contained by the exact full
remote-tracking ref
.IR REF .
Repeat for every containing ref. This option does not authorize a push or
force-push.
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON operation result.
.SH CONTINUE, ABORT, AND VERIFY OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-porcelain
Print a versioned JSON operation or verification result.
.SH PLAN CONTRACT
Only
.B plan.partitioned_units
and
.B plan.outputs
are semantic input. The
.B snapshot
object is immutable and is compared exactly after reacquisition. The
.B safety
object is informational and is always recollected when a plan is validated.
.PP
Every output contains a semantic operation, an EXACT or RESOLVED
materialization, one or more source commit IDs, an exact ordered source-unit
list, a message and its declared encoding, the exact target author, and an
optional human rationale. Rationale prose is not treated as mechanical proof.
An ordinary unit must occur exactly once globally. A declared partitioned unit
occurs once in each listed RESOLVED output, and nowhere else, when one
mechanical unit contains semantic portions owned by several snapshots.
.PP
KEEP requires the original message. REWORD permits a new message but preserves
the exact source patch, order, and author. SPLIT assigns non-empty ordered unit
subsets from one source to at least two output commits, preserving that source
author. INTEGRATE consumes a complete target source plus ordered subsets from
later repair sources; one repair source may be partitioned across several
targets, and may retain a later residual SPLIT output. REORDER marks a complete
source moved earlier and preserves its exact message and encoding.
.PP
Every earlier EXACT move must stay within the unit's recorded
adjacent-swap proof.
A chain of BLOCKED predecessors may move farther only when the complete chain
stays ordered inside one output; full-plan replay must then prove that compound
movement. A blocker assigned to another output and every UNKNOWN barrier fail
closed for exact replay. RESOLVED never claims commutation; it requires
explicit, path-authorized output snapshots and a complete replay that
reproduces the frozen final tree. Dependency evidence is not a substitute for
that final oracle. Empty source commits remain explicit unless deliberately
consumed by an integration.
.SH SAFETY
Scan, validation, and resolution do not change commits or refs. They
materialize candidate trees only in a temporary object quarantine. They may
run while local mutation preconditions are blocked and label those blockers in
the safety report. They reject merges, stale or forged snapshots, duplicate
JSON fields, abbreviated object IDs, omitted or unproven reordered units, and
active replace objects or legacy grafts. Scan compares the index directly to
the frozen final tree and never retains candidate objects in the repository's
object database.
.PP
Commands that acquire the frozen source snapshot may reuse immutable snapshot
and dependency analysis from a persistent JSON cache with at most 64 entries.
A cache hit requires an exact
repository, range, tree, branch, object-format, Git-behavior, configuration,
analysis-version, source-diff, and source-object-closure match. A miss or
failed check rebuilds the analysis from source objects. Live safety facts are
always collected again. The cache stores no candidate Git objects and never
substitutes for plan replay or final-tree proof.
.PP
The safety report records tracked index/worktree cleanliness, staging-session
and saved-batch state, active Git/rewrite operations, upstream divergence,
remote refs containing every source commit, and untracked-path count. A remote
ref containing any selected commit blocks mutation unless a later apply
invocation records that exact verified review-head exception. Untracked paths
are reported but do not by themselves block an object-based rewrite whose
final tree is unchanged.
.PP
Source cryptographic signature headers are identified by header name and
content digest. Their payloads are not copied into the plan. Rewritten commit
objects cannot retain the validity of source signatures, which is reported
explicitly.
.PP
Durable operation checkpoints live below
.B git-stage-batch/rewrite/
in the repository's common Git directory. That path is
.B .git/git-stage-batch/rewrite/
in an ordinary checkout, and remains reachable after a linked worktree is
removed.
They use private atomically replaced files, a closed phase/next-action schema,
operation-owned resolution copies when required, and operation-owned
recovery/output refs. A pending commit/tree checkpoint closes the crash window
around each output-ref update. Replacement objects are built in a quarantine,
promoted under a durable lease, and checked as a complete persistent closure
before branch publication. A deterministic verification record binds final
tree, output topology, plan digest, resolution provenance when present, and
removed signature digests. Status verifies the branch, expected tip, index,
worktree, plan, resolution and verification digests, output objects, recovery
ref, and the output ref that keeps a paused replacement chain reachable before
declaring resume ready.
Manual recovery output uses compare-and-swap
.B git update-ref
syntax so unrelated ref movement is not overwritten.
.PP
Completed and aborted operations deliberately retain their state and any refs
below
.BR refs/git-stage-batch/rewrite/ .
The command does not prune them because the refs preserve original and output
histories for later status, verification, and recovery. Inspect accumulated
refs with:
.PP
.B git for-each-ref --format='%(refname) %(objectname)' refs/git-stage-batch/rewrite/
.PP
After all terminal-operation recovery and verification needs have ended,
delete each reviewed ref with its exact expected object:
.PP
.B git update-ref -d EXACT_OPERATION_ORIGINAL_REF EXPECTED_ORIGINAL_HEAD
.PP
.B git update-ref -d EXACT_OPERATION_OUTPUT_REF EXPECTED_OUTPUT_HEAD
.PP
A partially built operation might not have an output ref. Removing an anchor
prevents later verification or automatic recovery for that operation and may
allow its commits to be garbage-collected.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git-stage-batch (1),
.BR git-stage-batch-fixup (1),
.BR git-commit (1),
.BR git-rev-list (1),
.BR git-update-ref (1)
